markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Prompting Large Language Models

You may be familiar with one or more of the following:

<div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;width:95%;margin:20pt auto;padding:0 5px;">
<div style="width:20%;text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/c22ec02e808ca32157566d9fb91efbe0-Screenshot%202026-01-29%20at%2011.40.54%20AM.png" alt="ChatGPT Logo" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto !important;max-height:90px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:5pt;margin:0 auto;">
</div>
<div style="width:20%;text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/c2897744ec844dadc387c770574cbefb-Screenshot%202026-01-29%20at%2011.39.33%20AM.png" alt="Gemini Logo" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto !important;max-height:90px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:5pt;margin:0 auto;">
</div>
<div style="width:20%;text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/4ac56eafff87360544c03cc012c9bd4a-Screenshot%202026-01-29%20at%2011.40.17%20AM.png" alt="Claude Logo" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto !important;max-height:90px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:5pt;margin:0 auto;">
</div>
<div style="width:20%;text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/bbfada5f0e117d70f873c77c7271e7eb-images.jpg" alt="Copilot Logo" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto !important;max-height:90px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:5pt;margin:0 auto;">
</div>
</div>

All of these are:
* Generative AI tools
* Built using Large Language Models (LLMs)
* Accessed through an AI chatbot interface

Most consumer AI tools, including all of the above, are now **multimodal**, meaning they can work with more than just text. They can understand and create content using text, images, audio, and sometimes video.

Users interact with these tools by writing **prompts** in an AI chat interface. How you word and structure your prompt will have a large effect on the output produced by the LLM.



## Practice

<img src="https://images.code.org/ef45b482f16ccd1f8a437f0c963f7c04-585015d9-e611-4704-b06e-83b52f8be36b.png" alt="Propmting tips and tricks. Ask follow-up questions. Be specific. Use feedback. Keep a critical eye and fact check." width="55%" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 10px 40px; border: 3px solid black;">


Now Let's try it it out! You'll use a Large Language Model (LLM) to improve your teaching practice.

To get you started, we have a small number of pre-developed prompts for you to use. Later in this module you'll get the chance to try additional prompts and to write your own.

Review the prompting tips and tricks before you get started.

## Educator’s Choice

1. Choose an LLM, such as <a href="https://chat.openai.com/" target="_blank">ChatGPT</a>, <a href="https://www.bing.com/chat" target="_blank">Copilot</a>, or <a href="https://gemini.google.com/" target="_blank">Gemini</a>, to prompt.
2. Select one of the prompts from the Prompt Library below.
3. 👉 Click on the prompt title to reveal the entire prompt. Copy and paste it into the LLM of your choice. 
4. Personalize the prompt to fit a specific task you are trying to complete. We recommend that you choose a realistic scenario from your classroom. <br><br> *Prompts have [ ] for you to fill in with specific instructions and context from your classroom.*
5. Try it out! 

## Prompt Library

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Differentiating an assessment</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an experienced teacher and instructional designer.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I am creating a formative assessment on <strong>[topic/standard]</strong>.
Here is my original assessment:
<strong>[Paste or describe the assessment]</strong>
My goal is to differentiate this assessment so all students can access the same learning goal:
<strong>[learning objective/standard]</strong>
Please create:
- A scaffolded version (for students who need support)
- A grade-level version
- An extension version (for advanced learners)
For each version:
- Keep it aligned to the same learning objective
- Adjust language, structure, and supports as needed
- Include clear student directions
- Maintain meaningful thinking (not just simplification)
Also include:
- A short explanation of how each version supports different learners
- Suggested supports (sentence starters, vocabulary, graphic organizers, etc.)
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Writing a parent email</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an experienced teacher helping draft communication with families.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I need to write an email to a parent/guardian about:
<strong>[purpose of the email]</strong>
Here are the key points to include:
- <strong>[Key point 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 2]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 3]</strong>
Additional context (tone, relationship, sensitivity, etc.):
<strong>[optional context]</strong>
Please write an email that:
- Is clear, concise, and easy to understand
- Uses a respectful, supportive, and professional tone
- Reflects collaboration with families
Then provide:
- 1–2 alternative ways to phrase key parts of the message
- Suggestions to improve clarity or tone if needed
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement
</strong>: Getting feedback on a lesson plan
</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an instructional coach providing actionable feedback.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I want feedback on a lesson about:
<strong>[lesson topic]</strong>
Here is my lesson plan:
<strong>[Paste or describe the lesson]</strong>
My goals for the lesson:
- <strong>[Goal 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Goal 2]</strong>
After teaching it, I noticed:
<strong>[what worked / what didn’t / student challenges]</strong>
Please give feedback on:
- Alignment to the learning goals
- Clarity of instructions
- Level of rigor and student thinking
- Opportunities for differentiation
Then suggest:
- Specific improvements I could make
- 1–2 alternative strategies to address student challenges
Keep feedback practical and focused on changes I can realistically implement.
</div>
</details>


<div style="clear:both;"></div>

> **💬 Quick Reflection:** Take a look at the prompts. What different parts of them do you notice?
MARKDOWN
